holds whenever \(|p+q|\ge2\).  

The case \(|p+q|=1\) is treated separately. The solution correctly observes that \(p+q=1\) forces \(q\ge2\) (otherwise \(p=0\) would give a horizontal line). It then argues that because the \(x\)-coordinate changes by steps of size \(q\ge2\), at most \(\lfloor(k/q)\rfloor+1\le\lfloor(k+1)/2\rfloor\) integer points can lie in the triangle. The same reasoning is claimed for \(p+q=-1\), but the argument is not spelled out; a rigorous justification would need to consider both the \(x\)-step size \(q\) and the fact that the sum decreases by 1 each step. Although the bound is indeed true (the worst case occurs for the line of slope \(-2\) where \(q=1\)), the solution does not provide the detailed argument for this sub‑case.  

